    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the tenure-gated membership level 4, keeping the change small and well-placed.\n\n**What \"level 4\" means here:** membership level is a derived field computed additively in `OwnerMapper.membershipLevel` (base 1, +1 for email, +1 for `namesakeCount == 0`). The pre-tenure factors already max out at 3, so level 4 needed a new tenure factor. The test comment confirms this: \"an email and no namesake maxes the pre-tenure factors, and must land at level 3, not 4.\"\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New `Tenure` domain helper** (`model/Tenure.java`) \u2014 a single-responsibility class mirroring the existing `AgeBand`/`CustomerCode` convention. `Tenure.days(registrationDate)` returns whole days from registration to today, clamping unknown/future dates to 0 so they can never satisfy a threshold.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.membershipLevel`** \u2014 added `+1 when Tenure.days(...) > 365` and raised the cap to 4. A new owner has `registrationDate == today` \u2192 0 tenure \u2192 the tenure factor never applies \u2192 capped at level 3. So a new owner with an email, `namesakeCount == 0` (and any household) is level 3, never 4.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `membershipLevel` schema (range/description now 1\u20134, tenure-gated top level) to stay consistent with the code.\n\nThe membership-rule logic stays in the mapper where the other derived fields live; the reusable tenure computation lives in its own domain class rather than being inlined.",
